  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1243 PM MDT Sat Aug 22 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nHigh amplitude upper level ridging is slipping east of the region \nthis afternoon ahead of an upper level trough moving onshore across \nthe Pacific NW. Resulting flow aloft is southwesterly, which in \ncombination with an embedded wave lifting northeastward from the base \nof the upper trough will result in quite a few showers and \nthunderstorms today. This activity looks to develop across SW MT \ninitially, spreading northeastward into Central MT and vicinity with \ntime. PWATs are elevated, signaling the risk for some heavy \nrainfall in the strongest thunderstorms. Modest inverted-V profiles \nsuggest strong wind gusts will be the primary concern from a severe \nweather perspective today, should thunderstorms be able to congeal \ninto at least a broken line. Looking toward tonight forcing \nincreases, especially west of the Continental Divide, as the core of \nthe upper trough approaches the Northern Rockies. There looks to be \nquite a bit of showers or thunderstorms that develop west of the \nContinental Divide as a result, with a few of these leaking east of \nthe divide through the overnight. \n\nForcing and flow aloft increases further for Sunday as the upper \nlevel system moves overhead and eventually east of the region Sunday \nnight. A cold front moves through associated with this system, \nbringing cooler temperatures, much drier air, and gusty winds. Ahead \nof this front there looks to be ample instability, forcing, and \nmarginal kinematics to result in at least a few loosely organized \nthunderstorms Sunday afternoon. Both strong wind gusts and large \nhail will be a concern in the most robust thunderstorms. This \nactivity largely looks to develop east of I-15 and continue eastward \nSunday. \n\nHeading into next week the trend is for weak ridging now across the \nNW CONUS, which will keep a slightly anticyclonic flow aloft in \nplace. This looks to inhibit showers and thunderstorms for a day or \ntwo in most areas, though monsoonal moisture stuck in the southern \nperiphery of the ridge will still result in a few showers and \nthunderstorms in SW MT near the Idaho border. The ridge axis slowly \nslips east through the week, with flow aloft becoming slightly more \ncyclonic each day as another Pacific trough begins to approach the \nPacific NW. Showers and thunderstorms gradually increase in coverage \nmid-week onwards, focused mostly in the afternoon and evening. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThunderstorms today:\n\nClearing is being noted on latest satellite imagery across much of \nID and into SW MT. This will result in at least weak instability \nthrough the remainder of the afternoon and evening. The degree to \nwhich the area destabilizes will play a large role in how widespread \nthe risk will be for strong wind gusts. Longer periods of sunny \nskies would increase this risk over shorter periods of sunshine. \n\nPrecipitable water (PWAT) values near and in excess of an inch will \nyield at least a localized risk for briefly heavy rainfall today in \nthe strongest thunderstorms. Should a strong thunderstorm move \nacross an urban area or a burn scar, excessive rainfall leading to \nflash flooding will be a concern. Overall, the risk for this across \nany specific area is too low to warrant any flash flood watches. \nAlthough this is the case, those in urban areas and near burn scars \nshould be aware of this risk today. \n\n\nThunderstorms Sunday: \n\nStronger flow aloft arrives with he core of the upper level \ndisturbance ahead of the cold front Sunday. Lingering moisture ahead \nof the cold front looks to combine with daytime destabilization to \nresult in modest instability by the early afternoon across many \nareas. Forcing does not look to be lacking Sunday, with the primary \nlimiting factor on organized thunderstorms being slightly sub-\noptimal shear (Closer to 30kts than 40kts). That said, at least \nloosely organized thunderstorms look to develop Sunday, mainly east \nof I-15, which will be capable of strong wind gusts and large hail. \nShould shear trend much higher, concern would increase for longer \nlasting, more organized severe thunderstorms. \n\n\nMid to late week:\n\nGuidance has been inconsistent with respect to the progression of \nPacific troughing moving in mid to late week. There is a roughly \n50/50 split in cluster guidance members for above average heights \n(Warmer temps) vs below average heights (Cooler temps) across the \nregion by the end of the week. -AM",
